{"receipt_id":"receipt_bef63848fc8b41ba848950d39756004e","provider_request_id":null,"attempt_id":"attempt_43a5bd36252d485a86c5a14b904a1714","neuron_id":"neuron_453d68c97608410a873ab48223656b24","role_id":"REASONING","adapter":"kilo-gateway","provider":"kilo-gateway","model":"poolside/laguna-s-2.1:free","model_family":"laguna","credential_handle":null,"started_at":"2026-10-14T04:29:24.080911Z","finished_at":"2026-10-14T04:29:30.404003Z","success":true,"output":"I need to verify the claim about PEP 703 and the free-threaded build of Python. Let me reason through this carefully.\n\nPEP 703 is titled \"Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional.\" The key facts I need to verify are:\n\n1. Whether Python 3.12 was the first version to include an experimental free-threaded build\n2. Whether PEP 703 first shipped in Python 3.12\n\nBased on my knowledge:\n\n- PEP 703 was accepted, but the experimental free-threaded build (\"nogil\" or free-threading) was not actually included in Python 3.12.\n- Python 3.13 was the version that first shipped with the experimental free-threaded build as described in PEP 703.\n- The free-threaded build was introduced as an experimental feature in Python 3.13, not 3.12.\n\nTherefore, the claim that PEP 703 \"first shipped as an experimental free-threaded build in Python 3.12\" is incorrect. The experimental free-threaded build first shipped in Python 3.13.\n\nVERDICT: REFUTED\nKEY: 3.13","failure_class":null,"failure_message":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":210,"output_tokens":252,"total_tokens":462,"cost_usd":0.0},"fallback_level":0,"metadata":{"status_code":200,"protocol":"OPENAI_CHAT","rate_headers":{},"provider_reported_model":"poolside/laguna-s-2.1:free","system_fingerprint":null,"finish_reason":"stop","routed_via":null,"effective_skill_ids":["reasoning"],"required_skill_ids":[],"authority_lease_id":"lease_233e8844a8d44eff8356cfa44ac7a8fb","skill_prompt_sha256":"5c557a00dfeb1b3f8638a4e6d71bab2d1aef306bec5b4dad5ee15b23971d945d","requested_mode